// @run
// @log CLOSURE
// The annotation on `v` is redundant to inference and kept deliberately: this file is about
// a DECLARED arm and an INFERRED one agreeing, so the declaration is the subject.
// @hint Intentionally-unused parameter `_q0`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `v` is inferred as
// GRADUATED from `xfail-miscompile-closure-arm-narrow-tag-disagreement.vl`, which pinned a
// SILENT WRONG ANSWER: `t0` holds the closure arm and `t0 is <that closure type>` answered
// FALSE, printing `OTHER`. It prints `CLOSURE` now.
//
// THE TAGS, read off the disassembly on both sides of the fix. The producer boxed the
// closure with `i32.const 12` and the `is` compared `i32.const 16` — 12 is
// `scalarTagOfKind(11)` (`cloSigSlotTag`'s slot 0) and 16 is `cloSigSlotTag(1)`. Both are
// `i32.const 12` now, and the lambda's own functype result went from `(ref $reflist)` to
// `(ref $uBox)`, so the narrowed `call_indirect` matches it too.
//
// THE MECHANISM, in the order the causes stack:
//   1. `internFuncTypeShapesTy` had NO composite-union arm. A closure RESULT that is a
//      value union (`{f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}`) fell past every arm of its grammar, so the
//      element's inline shape never interned during `collectAnnShapes` — and interning it
//      THERE is what keeps `collectAnonShapes` from naming the literal's shape `#anonN`
//      instead. With `#anon0` interned, the union arm's own `{f: boolean}[]` spelling
//      matched no reflist slot and the return-seam coerce refused: `array value does not
//      match any array member of the union`.
//   2. Because of (1), `refArmUnionRetName` DECLINED an anonymous array element beside an
//      object sibling — the decline existed to dodge that reject. So `synthRetAnnots` never
//      pinned this lambda's `fnRet`, it self-inferred `(i32) => {f: boolean}[]`, and
//      `cloBoxArmTy` derived the box tag from that while `isArmTagOfTy` derived the test tag
//      from the DECLARED arm. Two `cloSigTagNames` slots, one value.
// Fixing (1) let (2) be lifted, and the two tags now come from one render of one type.
//
// THE FAMILY THIS UNBLOCKED, each of which was a REJECT before and runs now:
// `narrow-reflist-arm-of-closure-result-union.vl` (the same shape with the inner narrow —
// it prints `false`, and the rep-fuzz baseline's last line graduated with it), the same
// union with a SCALAR sibling instead of an object one, and the annotated closure-typed
// binding `const f: (i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32} = …`, which used to answer
// `value-union closure RESULT is not yet representable`.
//
// STILL A CLEAN CHECKER REJECT, and deliberately so: `t0 is () => {f: boolean}[]` — a type
// that is not a member of the union at all — is refused by the type checker, not answered.

function makeIt(): ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64 {
  return (_q0) => ([{ f: true }])
}
const v: ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64 = makeIt()
const t0 = v
if t0 is (i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32} {
  print("CLOSURE")
} else {
  print("OTHER")
}
